ef 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186286124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186287102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186287126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186286124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287656 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb288b6a-0ce0-5325-bbb2-0ee9ec1811d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/32168a2cb1fe14dbc88725347611ae5951198e81dfaa0b41e780106750bbb741.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,3x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "281f8e94-72c2-5b73-8bcb-5cb4814a0f37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287725 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=3x$ and *below* the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>3x,\\,y<\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n3x<y<\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n3x\\le y<\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3x\\le y<\\frac{x}{4}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/98/980ae73286ed41ed0c6f074a4bed5b35fa852dd608fb0a1a561f26a7eac60ea7.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8779b713-2ad1-53d1-a3e6-394f41bc52c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287798 #edb/ref 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *to the left* the line $x=2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx<2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\nx<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,x<2\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218